  "issue_title": "Prefork: EOFError: Ran out of input from _help_stuff_finish at exit when pool children get SIGTERM during warm shutdown",
  "issue_body": "<!--\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\nbug reports which are incomplete.\n-->\n# Checklist\n<!--\nTo check an item on the list replace [ ] with [x].\n-->\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n    (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n     version affected).\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n<!--\nTry some of the below if you think they are relevant.\nIt will help us figure out the scope of the bug and how many users it affects.\n-->\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\nNotes on the boxes above:\n- Python 3.14.8 and 3.13.16, CPython only.\n- Redis 7.0.15 locally and 7.4 (Docker `redis:7.4-alpine`, Azure Managed Redis). Only Redis was tried, but the broker isn't involved: the failing read is on the prefork pool's own pipes.\n- Linux only. The bug is specific to the prefork pool (`AsynPool`), so other pools don't apply.\n- The reproduction app has no tasks, autoscaling, retries, ETA or rate limits.\n- Reproduced on celery 5.6.3 and `main` (155a8f7, 5.7.0b1), and on billiard 4.3.0 and 4.3.1.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n<!--\nPlease make sure to search and mention any related issues\nor possible duplicates to this issue as requested by the checklist above.\n\nThis may or may not include issues in other repositories that the Celery project\nmaintains or other repositories that are dependencies of Celery.\n\nIf you don't know how to mention issues, please refer to Github's documentation\non the subject: https://help.github.com/en/articles/autolinked-references-and-urls#issues-and-pull-requests\n-->\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- celery/billiard#434 and celery/billiard#435 changed `Pool._terminate_pool`'s shutdown sequence, which is where this traceback starts. They don't touch `_help_stuff_finish`.\n- billiard 4.3.1, released 2026-10-05, also fixes a related problem on the same platforms. A child that receives SIGTERM while running a task no longer reports it as failed, so with `task_acks_late` the task is requeued (`WorkerLostError`) instead of acknowledged and lost. That release doesn't change the EOFError below.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None. #5013 mentions `_help_stuff_finish` only as a debug log line in an unrelated, unexplained worker exit. #3780 and #3559 (\"Ran out of input\") are Windows spawn pickling errors.\n\n## Environment & Settings\n<!-- Include the contents of celery --version below -->\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0b1 (collider), installed from `main` at 155a8f720b01ebd7999b34e525c121b8f824ac73; also 5.6.3 (recovery)\n<!-- Include the output of celery -A proj report below -->\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0b1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0b1 py:3.14.8\n            billiard:4.3.1 redis:8.1.0\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.18.44-fc-v114 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:redis results:disabled\n\nbroker_url: 'redis://:********@localhost:6390/0'\ndeprecated_settings: None\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n<!-- Please fill the required dependencies to reproduce this issue -->\n- **Minimal Python Version**: Unknown (reproduced on 3.13.16 and 3.14.8)\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: Unknown (reproduced on 5.6.3 and `main`)\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: Unknown (reproduced on 5.6.2 and 5.7.0b1)\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A (reproduced on Redis 7.0.15 and 7.4; the broker isn't involved)\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A (results disabled)\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: Unknown (Linux 6.18; also Debian trixie containers, `python:3.14.8-slim`)\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A (redis-py 6.4.0 and 8.1.0)\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A\n\n### Python Packages\n<!-- Please fill the contents of pip freeze below -->\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.1\nbilliard==4.3.1\ncelery @ git+https://github.com/celery/celery@155a8f720b01ebd7999b34e525c121b8f824ac73\nclick==8.5.0\nclick-didyoumean==0.3.1\nclick-plugins==1.1.1.2\nclick-repl==0.4.1\nkombu==5.7.0b1\npackaging==26.3\nprompt-toolkit==3.0.53\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\nredis==8.1.0\nsix==1.17.0\ntyping-extensions==4.16.0\ntzdata==2026.4\ntzlocal==5.4.4\nvine==5.1.0\nwcwidth==0.9.2\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n<!--\nPlease provide system dependencies, configuration files\nand other dependency information if applicable\n-->\n<details>\n<p>\nA Redis server, used only as the broker: `redis-server --port 6390 --save ''`. No configuration files.\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n<!--\nPlease provide a reproducible test case.\nRefer to the Reporting Bugs section in our contribution guide.\n\nWe prefer submitting test cases in the form of a PR to our integration test suite.\nIf you can provide one, please mention the PR number below.\nIf not, please attach the most minimal code example required to reproduce the issue below.\nIf the test case is too large, please include a link to a gist or a repository below.\n\nAlternatively, the pytest-celery plugin can be used to create standalone reproduction scripts\nthat can be added to this report. See the pytest-celery documentation for more information at\npytest-celery.readthedocs.io\n-->\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\nSome container platforms deliver SIGTERM to every process in the container; we hit this on Azure Container Apps. The script stands in for that by sending SIGTERM to the pool children from `AsynPool._stop_task_handler`, just after warm shutdown's `Pool.join()` has written each child's `None` sentinel.\n\n```python\n# repro_app.py\nimport os\nimport signal\n\nfrom celery import Celery\nfrom celery.concurrency import asynpool\n\napp = Celery(\"repro\", broker=os.environ.get(\"BROKER\", \"redis://localhost:6379/0\"))\n\n_original = asynpool.AsynPool._stop_task_handler\n_joined = []\n\n\ndef _stop_task_handler(task_handler):\n    _original(task_handler)  # writes the 8-byte None sentinel to each child's inqueue\n\n    if not _joined:  # only the warm-shutdown join, not the atexit finalizer's second call\n        _joined.append(True)\n\n        for proc in task_handler.pool:\n            os.kill(proc.pid, signal.SIGTERM)\n\n\nasynpool.AsynPool._stop_task_handler = staticmethod(_stop_task_handler)\n```\n\n```bash\nredis-server --port 6390 --save '' --daemonize yes\nBROKER=redis://localhost:6390/0 celery -A repro_app worker --concurrency=2 --loglevel=warning & pid=$!\nsleep 4; kill -TERM $pid; wait $pid; echo \"exit $?\"\n```\n\nResult: the traceback below in 5 of 5 runs on `main` with Python 3.14.8, and in 5 of 5 runs on celery 5.6.3 with Python 3.13.16.\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Expected Behavior\n<!-- Describe in detail what you expect to happen -->\n\nThe warm shutdown completes and the worker exits without a traceback. The pool has already been closed and joined, and every child has exited, so nothing in a child's inqueue needs draining.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n<!--\nDescribe in detail what actually happened.\nPlease include a backtrace and surround it with triple backticks (```).\nIn addition, include the Celery daemon logs, the broker logs,\nthe result backend logs and system logs below if they will help us debug\nthe issue.\n-->\n\nAbout 2 seconds after `worker: Warm shutdown (MainProcess)`, the worker prints the following (from `main`). The process still exits 0, because `multiprocessing.util._run_finalizers` catches and prints the exception. But on stderr it reads as an error, and log pipelines report it on every deploy.\n\n```\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \".../lib/python3.14/multiprocessing/util.py\", line 371, in _run_finalizers\n    finalizer()\n    ~~~~~~~~~^^\n  File \".../lib/python3.14/multiprocessing/util.py\", line 295, in __call__\n    res = self._callback(*self._args, **self._kwargs)\n  File \".../site-packages/billiard/pool.py\", line 1738, in _terminate_pool\n    cls._help_stuff_finish(*help_stuff_finish_args)\n    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \".../site-packages/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py\", line 1484, in _help_stuff_finish\n    fileno_to_proc[fd].inq._reader.recv()\n    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^\n  File \".../site-packages/billiard/connection.py\", line 276, in recv\n    return ForkingPickler.loadbuf(buf)\n           ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^\n  File \".../site-packages/billiard/reduction.py\", line 60, in loadbuf\n    return cls.loads(buf.getbuffer())\n           ~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nEOFError: Ran out of input\n```\n\n### Cause\n\n1. Warm shutdown runs `TaskPool.on_stop` \u2192 `Pool.close()` + `Pool.join()`. `join()` calls `AsynPool._stop_task_handler`, which writes a `None` sentinel to each child's inqueue: a 4-byte `!i` length followed by the 4-byte pickle `b'\\x80\\x05N.'`.\n2. A child that receives SIGTERM as it wakes to read the sentinel can read the 4-byte header before its terminate handler (`billiard.common._shutdown_cleanup`) runs. Linux's `pipe_read` returns available data before checking for pending signals, and Python runs the handler at the next bytecode boundary, between `_recv(4)` and `_recv(size)`. The child exits and the 4-byte body stays in the pipe.\n3. `on_stop` never calls `Pool.terminate()`, so the pool's `Finalize(_terminate_pool, exitpriority=15)` runs at interpreter exit, on the already-joined pool. `_help_stuff_finish` selects on every child's inqueue, including those of children that have exited, and calls `recv()` on the readable one.\n4. `recv()` reads `b'\\x80\\x05N.'` as the length header (-2147135954), so `_recv(size)` returns an empty buffer and `pickle.loads(b'')` raises `EOFError: Ran out of input`.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nSkip children that have already exited, since nothing will ever read their inqueues again:\n\n```python\n    @classmethod\n    def _help_stuff_finish(cls, pool):\n        ...\n        for w in pool:\n            if w.exitcode is not None:\n                continue  # nothing will ever read this inqueue again\n            try:\n                fd = w.inq._reader.fileno()\n```\n\nWith this applied as a monkeypatch to the reproduction above, the traceback went away: 0 of 5 runs, exit code 0.\n\nTwo alternatives, both untested: `TaskPool.on_stop` could cancel the pool's finalizer (`self._pool._terminate.cancel()`) once `join()` returns, or billiard's `Pool.join()` could do the same. I'm happy to open a PR for whichever approach you prefer.\n",
